# Coding Standards

## TypeScript

- `strict` mode everywhere. No implicit `any`. Prefer explicit public types on exports.
- No `// @ts-ignore` without a justification comment; prefer `@ts-expect-error` with reason.

## Naming

- Packages: `@azes/<name>` (kebab-case), e.g. `@azes/ui-web`, `@azes/design-tokens`.
- Apps: kebab-case directory names matching the target list.
- Files: React components `PascalCase.tsx`; hooks `useX.ts`; utilities `camelCase.ts`;
  test files `*.test.ts(x)`; config files as the tool expects.
- Variables/functions `camelCase`; types/interfaces/enums `PascalCase`; constants `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`.

## Comments

- Do not add comments that merely narrate code. Explain intent, trade-offs, or constraints.
- Every comment-supporting file carries the AZES header — see `comment-header.mdc`.

## Quality

- After edits, ensure lint/typecheck pass for the touched scope. Fix lint errors you introduce.
- No `console.log` in app/package source; use `@azes/logger` once that package exists.
- Never commit secrets. Use environment configuration (see `docs/architecture/security.md`).
- Do not introduce unnecessary dependencies. Justify bundle/runtime/maintenance impact.
